import{_ as a,a as t,c as r,j as n}from"./chunks/framework.DVzkjf-b.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"From Personalized Knowledge Artifacts to Governed AI Systems","description":"Why generating personalized educational, business, and media artifacts led me to research provenance, multimodal evidence, and human attention.","frontmatter":{"title":"From Personalized Knowledge Artifacts to Governed AI Systems","description":"Why generating personalized educational, business, and media artifacts led me to research provenance, multimodal evidence, and human attention."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research/systems/personalized-knowledge-artifacts.md","filePath":"research/systems/personalized-knowledge-artifacts.md"}'),o={name:"research/systems/personalized-knowledge-artifacts.md"};function i(s,e,d,l,c,h){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="from-personalized-knowledge-artifacts-to-governed-ai-systems" tabindex="-1">From Personalized Knowledge Artifacts to Governed AI Systems <a class="header-anchor" href="#from-personalized-knowledge-artifacts-to-governed-ai-systems" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;From Personalized Knowledge Artifacts to Governed AI Systems&quot;">​</a></h1><p><em>Research essay · November 2026</em></p><p>I began with an application question: <strong>could AI make an individually useful knowledge artifact as practical to produce as a mass-market document?</strong></p><p>An artifact might be a lesson for one learner, an audio explanation for a commuter, a business brief tailored to an operator, a research video, or a structured packet for another agent. Personalization would change the presentation and level of detail. It should not change what the underlying evidence supports.</p><p>The challenge quickly moved beyond generation. A compelling artifact can still be wrong. A technically correct artifact can fail a human need. And checking every sentence or frame with a specialist destroys the economics of producing artifacts individually.</p><h2 id="the-engineering-problem-behind-the-product" tabindex="-1">The engineering problem behind the product <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-engineering-problem-behind-the-product" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The engineering problem behind the product&quot;">​</a></h2><p>To generate repeatedly from mixed sources, a system needs to know:</p><ul><li>Where a statement came from and when the evidence was obtained.</li><li>Whether it is a first-hand observation, quotation, inference, allegation, or independently supported claim.</li><li>Which identity or event a passage refers to, including unresolved alternatives.</li><li>Which transformations produced a summary or generated asset.</li><li>What a human or authorized agent approved, and <strong>the scope</strong> of that approval.</li><li>When new evidence should trigger reconsideration.</li></ul><p>This led me toward a shared knowledge foundation: retain originals and exact spans; derive claims and graph relations; index for lexical and semantic retrieval; qualify information for particular uses; and generate many forms of artifacts from the same governed sources.</p><p>The graph would act as a compressed map of the information, not a replacement for the original evidence. A user or agent could request the source passage whenever a consequential decision demanded it.</p><h2 id="how-my-projects-explored-this" tabindex="-1">How my projects explored this <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-my-projects-explored-this" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How my projects explored this&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>December 2024 — <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/chunky-monkey-text-chunker" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Chunky Monkey</a>.</strong> The repository records work on token-aware chunking, document metadata, content hashes, verification modes, and repeatable ingestion. This was one necessary infrastructure problem: keeping large information collections manageable and inspectable.</p><p><strong>December 2025 — <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/metavis-demo-projects" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">MetaVis</a>.</strong> I explored native Swift and Metal media processing, sensor observations, deterministic reference tests, and Gemini-based qualitative reviews. The architecture separated ambiguous interpretation from typed, reversible execution. The code and sprint records provide a richer account than a feature list.</p><p><strong>2026 — <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/ordo" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ordo</a> and Ordo Executor (private prototype).</strong> The focus moved toward model-agnostic tools, source manifests, evidence-bearing artifacts, graph candidates, explicit promotion, and risk-based human review. The main Ordo repository contains extensive specifications; its documented Elixir runtime is still planned.</p><p><strong>November 2026 — UAP Corpus (private research catalog).</strong> This is a deliberately difficult collection for testing shared-source repetition, contradictory accounts, provenance, and unknowns. The repository contains metadata and acquisition planning, not a completed downloaded corpus.</p><p>These dated repository milestones establish a progression of experimentation, not priority over all prior academic work. Commit dates can also postdate the first idea.</p><h2 id="what-i-mean-by-reliability" tabindex="-1">What I mean by reliability <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-i-mean-by-reliability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What I mean by reliability&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Not every artifact needs the same degree of review. Formatting a non-sensitive summary and approving a financial obligation have different consequences. A useful system should choose how much evidence and attention to spend, explain the decision, and retain the ability to recover from mistakes.</p><p>I therefore see the long-term system as <em>evidence-governed artifact production</em>: cheap operations where reliable, qualified AI review where appropriate, and accountable humans where authority or risk demands it.</p><h2 id="what-needs-testing" tabindex="-1">What needs testing <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-needs-testing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What needs testing&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The central hypothesis is that reusing qualified knowledge can reduce the <strong>marginal cost of producing an additional trustworthy personalized artifact</strong>, while maintaining citation fidelity and a defensible record of decisions.</p><p>A valid experiment needs independent reference labels, multiple datasets, measured human review minutes, computational cost, quality, contradiction preservation, and failure recovery. Building the platform alone would not prove that claim.</p><h3 id="connections-to-prior-research" tabindex="-1">Connections to prior research <a class="header-anchor" href="#connections-to-prior-research" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Connections to prior research&quot;">​</a></h3><p><a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/from-local-to-global-a-graph-rag-approach-to-query-focused-summarization/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GraphRAG (Edge et al., 2024)</a> already demonstrates graph-based corpus summarization. <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">W3C PROV-O (2013)</a> gives a reusable model for provenance. <a href="https://aisel.aisnet.org/misq/vol28/iss1/6/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hevner et al. (2004)</a> provides the design-science framework for learning through evaluated artifacts.</p><p>The research opportunity is in an evaluated combination, not in claiming that each component is new.</p><p><a href="./">Research series</a> · <a href="./dissertation-proposal">Dissertation proposal</a></p>',26)])])}const u=a(o,[["render",i]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
